-- $Id: README,v 1.3 2020/02/02 23:34:34 tom Exp $
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The functions in this directory are the generic (not device-specific) modules
of ncurses.

As a rule, these modules should not depend directly on term.h references and
associated terminfo function and variables.


A FIRST TOUR: WHAT THESE FILES DO IN THE RUNNING PROGRAM
========================================================

The easiest way to understand this directory is not as a collection of
independent functions, but as one path from an application's idea of a screen
to the real terminal, plus the reverse path for keyboard input.

A typical output path is:

    newwin()
        -> waddch(), waddstr(), etc.
        -> wrefresh()
        -> wnoutrefresh()
        -> doupdate()
        -> terminal cursor movement and character output

The important distinction is that most of the "base" routines operate on an
in-memory WINDOW.  They are not immediately writing escape sequences to the
terminal.

WINDOW, newwin(), and lib_newwin.c
----------------------------------

A WINDOW is an in-memory rectangular grid of screen cells plus bookkeeping:
its size, its position on the screen, its current logical cursor position,
attributes, background, scrolling region, and which cells have changed.

newwin(lines, columns, y, x) creates one of these rectangles.  It allocates the
cell storage and initially fills it with blank cells.  Creating a window does
not make anything appear on the terminal.

subwin() and derwin() create views into another window.  Unlike a completely
new window, a derived/subwindow can share the parent's cell storage.

So "new window" means "make a new editable screen buffer/rectangle", not
"open a new operating-system or graphical window".


addch, waddch(), and lib_addch.c
-------------------------------

"add character" means "put a character into a WINDOW at that window's logical
cursor position".

waddch(win, ch) is a central mutation operation.  It combines the character
with the window's current attributes/background, stores the resulting cell in
the window, advances or wraps the window cursor, handles scrolling when
needed, and marks the affected part of the window as changed.

It does not normally mean "write this byte to the terminal right now".
Much of the higher-level text-output API eventually reduces to operations like
waddch().

The initial `w` means that the operation takes an explicit WINDOW.  The plain
forms such as addch() conventionally operate on stdscr, the default window.


refresh, wrefresh(), wnoutrefresh(), and lib_refresh.c
------------------------------------------------------

A WINDOW can be edited many times before any terminal output occurs.
Refreshing is the bridge from those private window buffers toward the complete
screen image.

wnoutrefresh(win) copies the changed parts of a WINDOW into ncurses' desired
whole-screen image, called newscr.  This still does not necessarily perform
physical output.

wrefresh(win) normally performs that merge and then calls doupdate().
This separation lets several windows be merged into newscr first and then sent
to the terminal in one update.

The useful mental model is:

    WINDOW      = one application's rectangular view/buffer
    newscr      = what ncurses wants the whole terminal to look like
    curscr      = what ncurses believes the physical terminal looks like now


doupdate() and tty_update.c
---------------------------

The tty layer is where the abstract screen becomes terminal I/O.  "tty" is the
traditional Unix name for a terminal device; here it means the backend that
has to deal with the behavior of an actual terminal rather than merely editing
a WINDOW.

doupdate() compares newscr, the desired whole-screen image, with curscr, the
remembered current physical image.  It works out what has changed and emits the
terminal operations needed to make the real display match newscr.  Physical
screen output is concentrated in tty_update.c (apart from the lower-level
character-output helper used by tputs()).

This is why waddch() and doupdate() are very different operations even though
both ultimately contribute to putting a character on the display: waddch()
changes the desired data; doupdate() realizes those changes on the device.


mvcur and lib_mvcur.c
---------------------

"mvcur" means "move the physical terminal cursor".

It is easy to confuse this with changing a WINDOW's logical cursor.  They are
not the same thing.  A WINDOW may say that its next character belongs at row 7,
column 20 while the terminal's real cursor is currently somewhere else.
During doupdate(), the tty code calls the cursor-movement machinery when it
needs the physical terminal cursor at a particular location before emitting
text or another terminal operation.

lib_mvcur.c therefore sits underneath screen updating.  Given an old physical
cursor position and a required new one, it considers terminal capabilities such
as absolute cursor addressing and relative up/down/left/right movement and
chooses a sequence to emit.  Much of its complexity is historical optimization
for terminals where different movement sequences had materially different
transmission costs.

So, in context:

    move()/wmove()     changes a WINDOW's logical cursor
    mvcur()            moves the real terminal cursor while painting

Those names sound similar but belong to different layers.


getch, wgetch(), and lib_getch.c
--------------------------------

"get character" is the main input path, but it does substantially more than a
single read() of one byte.

wgetch(win) obtains the next input event associated with a curses window.  If
the window has pending visible changes, ncurses refreshes it before waiting for
input.  It then reads from the screen's input file descriptor, buffers bytes,
applies the requested blocking/timeout behavior, and, when keypad processing
is enabled, recognizes multi-byte terminal escape sequences and converts them
into curses key values such as arrow/function-key codes.

It also participates in mouse-event handling, resize handling, echo behavior,
and cooked/raw/cbreak input modes.

The contextual path is therefore approximately:

    terminal bytes/events
        -> input FIFO
        -> escape-sequence/key recognition
        -> wgetch()/getch() result for the application

Again, the initial `w` means an explicit WINDOW.  getch() is the default-window
form.


How the pieces fit together
---------------------------

For a small curses program, think of the cycle as:

    1. newwin() creates an editable rectangular buffer.
    2. waddch()/waddstr()/etc. change that buffer.
    3. wrefresh() merges its changes into the desired whole screen.
    4. doupdate() compares desired screen state with remembered physical state.
    5. mvcur() and related tty routines position the real cursor and emit the
       required terminal sequences and character data.
    6. wgetch() refreshes if necessary, waits for terminal input, decodes it,
       and returns an application-level character or key event.
    7. The program edits its WINDOWs in response, and the cycle repeats.

This distinction between "edit an in-memory screen" and "make the physical
terminal match it" is the central idea to keep in mind while reading the
source.